WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.560
Step eight.

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:02.942
Monitoring was switched on by the template.

00:00:02.992 --> 00:00:06.101
Now we break something, on purpose, and watch it surface.

00:00:06.151 --> 00:00:10.586
Two agents run in the cluster: AMA metrics scrapes Prometheus metrics,

00:00:10.636 --> 00:00:12.957
and AMA logs collects container logs.

00:00:13.007 --> 00:00:17.164
The alert rules are code too: a Prometheus rule group in the monitoring module.

00:00:17.214 --> 00:00:19.925
The demo scenarios workflow starts a load test,

00:00:19.975 --> 00:00:24.271
and then injects a fault: thirty percent of membership API calls now fail.

00:00:24.321 --> 00:00:28.382
In Log Analytics, the requests by service and status show the five-oh-threes from

00:00:28.432 --> 00:00:28.889
the fault.

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:34.434
Errors per five minutes jump right after the fault, at nineteen thirty four UTC.

00:00:34.484 --> 00:00:37.983
Seven minutes later, the high five x x rate alert fired,

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.947
once for each affected service.

00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:44.980
And resource use stays small under this load: a few millicores per pod.
